@startuml levenshtein-backends
' Backend selection decision tree. Every leaf is a libdictenstein backend that
' libgrammstein actually instantiates — except SuffixAutomatonChar, which exists
' but is deliberately not wired in (drawn grey to say so). The question order is
' the order that matters in practice: durability first (it constrains everything
' downstream), then mutability, then the read/space trade-off.
' Rectangles (not the activity beta) so the fills are exact and stable.
' Labels use Unicode glyphs, not <latex>: house style, see docs/diagrams/README.md.
' Render: plantuml -tsvg docs/diagrams/levenshtein-backends.puml
' Source of truth for Figure 1 in docs/integration/liblevenshtein/backend-selection.md §3.
skinparam backgroundColor #FAFAFA
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam defaultTextAlignment center
skinparam linetype ortho
skinparam nodesep 40
skinparam ranksep 45
skinparam rectangle {
  FontColor white
  FontStyle bold
  BorderColor #263238
  roundCorner 12
}
' Decisions are light-filled, so they need dark text — the global white would vanish.
skinparam rectangle<<decision>> {
  BackgroundColor #ECEFF1
  FontColor #263238
  FontStyle plain
  BorderColor #546E7A
}
skinparam ArrowColor #37474F
skinparam ArrowFontColor #37474F
skinparam NoteBackgroundColor #ECEFF1
skinparam NoteBorderColor #90A4AE
skinparam NoteFontColor #263238
title Choosing a dictionary backend — every leaf is a libdictenstein type

legend top right
  |= Color |= Concept |
  |<#ECEFF1>     | Decision |
  |<#00838F>     | Storage backend (wired into libgrammstein) |
  |<#546E7A>     | Available, deliberately not wired |
endlegend

rectangle "**Q1.** Corpus exceeds RAM,\nor must survive a crash?" as q1 <<decision>>
rectangle "**Q2.** Still being trained\n(needs writes)?" as q2 <<decision>>
rectangle "**Q3.** Want cheap snapshots /\nstructural sharing?" as q3 <<decision>>
rectangle "**Q4.** Match anywhere *inside* a term\n(substring), not from the start?" as q4 <<decision>>

rectangle "**PersistentARTrieChar<V>**\n""SharedCharARTrie<V> = Arc<PersistentARTrieChar<V>>""\nWAL + checkpoints · lock-free CAS overlay\nSIMD Node16 ladder · 𝒪(m) + I/O" as artrie #00838F
rectangle "**PathMapDictionary<V>**\nradix-256 trie DAG · shared subtries\nalias ""PathMapNgramModel""\nno serde — PathMap-native persistence" as pmd #00838F
rectangle "**DynamicDawgChar<V>**\nlock-free, wait-free exact traversal\nsuffix sharing · insert **and** remove\nalias ""SerializableNgramModel"" (serde)" as dawg #00838F
rectangle "**DoubleArrayTrieChar<V>**\nfastest lookup, most compact (≈8 B/node)\nbulk-built, read-only: ""MappedDictionary"" only\n""from_portable_static"" / ""load_static_portable""" as dat #00838F
rectangle "**SuffixAutomatonChar<V>**\nsubstring search (match anywhere)\nexists in libdictenstein —\nlibgrammstein does **not** use it" as sa #546E7A

q1 -down-> artrie : **yes**
q1 -down-> q2 : no — fits in memory
q2 -down-> q3 : **yes**
q2 -down-> q4 : no — model is frozen
q3 -down-> pmd : **yes**
q3 -down-> dawg : no — ordinary training
q4 -down-> sa : **yes**
q4 -down-> dat : no — whole-term lookup

note right of artrie
  The corpus-scale training backend (Google Books).
  Pair it with **PersistentVocabARTrie** for the
  term ↔ u64 vocabulary bijection — that, and not
  the DAWG, is where a Bloom filter is armed for
  fast out-of-vocabulary rejection.
end note

note right of dawg
  The default. Its ""auto_minimize_threshold"" and
  ""bloom_filter_capacity"" arguments are accepted
  for **API compatibility only** — the lock-free core
  ignores them and traverses exactly. They are not
  live tuning knobs; do not document them as such.
end note

note bottom of dat
  Every backend answers a lookup in 𝒪(m) for a term of
  length m, independent of the number of stored terms n.
  The choice therefore trades **build cost, mutability and space**
  — never asymptotic query time.
end note

@enduml
